<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope John XXII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1244–1334</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Jacques Duèze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope John XXII, born Jacques Duèze in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cahors">Cahors</a>, was pope from 1316 to 1334, the second of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> popes. He centralised papal finances and administration, fought a long conflict with the emperor Louis IV and condemned the Franciscan doctrine of absolute poverty. The Scottish barons addressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-arbroath">Declaration of Arbroath</a> to him in 1320, and he recognised Robert I as king in 1324.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii.jpg" alt="Pope John XXII" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown century 14th · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_XXII_1316.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
